WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 14 gave the roasting line: 3,400,000 out at the start, 1,050,000 a year for five years, and 550,000 more at the end. Chapter 15 gave the rate: 9 per cent for a project of ordinary risk, higher where the risk is unusual. Book 2's Chapter 1 gave the arithmetic of present value. Everything in this chapter uses those three.
16.1  Net present value
Net present value is the present value of a project's cash inflows less the present value of its outflows, all discounted at the hurdle rate. A positive figure means the project returns more than the money costs, so it creates value; a negative figure means it destroys value; zero means it exactly earns its cost of capital, which is a pass rather than a failure.
The computation has four steps, and the exam tests each of them.
Lay out the cash flows by year, as Chapter 14 did, using cash and not accounting profit.
Find the discount factor for each year. For a single amount in year n at rate r the factor is 1 ÷ (1 + r) to the power n. For a level amount in every year from 1 to n, the annuity factor is the sum of those factors, and using it saves four multiplications.
Multiply and add.
Subtract the initial investment, which needs no discounting because it happens now.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: the roasting line at 9 per cent
Step 1. The flows. Year 0: −3,400,000. Years 1 to 5: +1,050,000. Year 5 also: +550,000, being 400,000 of released working capital and 150,000 of after-tax salvage.
Step 2. The factors. At 9 per cent the five-year annuity factor is 3.8897 and the year-5 single-amount factor is 0.6499.
Step 3. The present values. The level flows are worth 1,050,000 × 3.8897 = 4,084,185. The terminal amount is worth 550,000 × 0.6499 = 357,445. Together 4,084,185 + 357,445 = 4,441,630.
Step 4. The net figure. 4,441,630 − 3,400,000 = 1,041,630, which is positive, so the line clears its hurdle.
Check. Read the result as a sentence: building this line makes Orontes about 1,041,630 better off today than not building it, after paying the providers of the money everything they require. That is the economic profit of Book 1 and the market value added of Book 2, computed in advance instead of after the fact.
Three readings of the figure are worth having.
It is additive. The net present value of two projects is the sum of their individual figures, which no rate-based measure can claim. That is why net present values can be aggregated into a capital budget and compared with the company's market value.
It is stated in money, so it reflects size. A project earning 40 per cent on 100,000 creates less value than one earning 12 per cent on 10,000,000, and only a money measure says so.
It is the shareholders' gain. In the theory of Book 2, announcing a project with a positive net present value should raise the company's market value by about that amount, which is the link between capital budgeting and the share price.
Figure F716-02 shows the computation laid out.
Figure F716-02. The roasting line discounted at 9 per cent
@table
Year | Cash flow | Factor at 9% | Present value
0 | (3,400,000) | 1.0000 | (3,400,000)
1–5 | 1,050,000 each | 3.8897 | 4,084,185
5 (terminal) | 550,000 | 0.6499 | 357,445
Net present value | | | 1,041,630
@end
