16.2  What net present value is good at, and what it is not
The outcome asks for advantages and limitations, and both lists are short enough to learn.
Its advantages. It uses all the cash flows, including those beyond any payback period. It discounts them, so timing is handled properly. It uses the cost of capital, so the required return is explicit and can be adjusted for risk as Chapter 15 showed. It measures in money, so size matters. It is additive. And it corresponds directly to value created for the providers of capital, which makes it the measure most consistent with the objective Book 2 set out.
Its limitations, and these are the ones candidates forget.
It depends entirely on the forecasts. A net present value computed to the nearest dollar from volumes guessed to the nearest hundred thousand jars has a precision it has not earned, which is why Chapter 15's sensitivity analysis belongs beside it.
It depends on the rate, and the rate is an estimate. Three points of hurdle rate removed a third of the roasting line's value in Chapter 15.
It is hard to explain to people who do not work with discounting. A rate or a payback period communicates immediately; a net present value usually needs a paragraph.
It gives no sense of scale relative to the investment. A net present value of 1,041,630 is excellent on 3,400,000 and trivial on 340,000,000, and Chapter 17's profitability index exists to fix exactly that.
It assumes the money is available. Where capital is rationed, the highest net present value is not necessarily the right choice, which again is Chapter 17's subject.
And it says nothing about the pattern of the flows, so it cannot distinguish a project that recovers its money quickly from one that does not — a difference that matters when a company's survival depends on liquidity, as Book 1's Chapter 11 made clear.
EXAM TRAP   The precision illusion
A question that gives a net present value to the dollar and asks whether to accept is testing arithmetic. A question that gives one and asks what else you would want to know is testing judgment, and the answer is always some combination of: the reliability of the volume and price forecasts, the basis of the discount rate, what happens if the key assumption is wrong, and what the project commits the company to that cannot be undone.
