16.3  The internal rate of return
The internal rate of return is the discount rate at which a project's net present value is exactly zero. It is the project's own return, computed from its cash flows and nothing else, which is where the word internal comes from: no external rate enters the calculation.
The decision rule is to accept where the internal rate of return exceeds the hurdle rate. For a conventional project — one outflow followed by inflows — that rule gives the same answer as net present value, because a positive net present value at the hurdle rate and an internal rate above it are two statements of one fact.
There is no formula for it. It is found by trial, by interpolation between two trials, or by a spreadsheet doing the same thing quickly. The exam expects the interpolation.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: the roasting line's internal rate of return
Step 1. Try 19 per cent. The five-year annuity factor is 3.0576 and the year-5 factor 0.4190, so the inflows are worth 1,050,000 × 3.0576 = 3,210,480 plus 550,000 × 0.4190 = 230,450, which is 3,210,480 + 230,450 = 3,440,930, giving a net present value of 3,440,930 − 3,400,000 = 40,930. Still positive, so the true rate is higher.
Step 2. Try 20 per cent. The factors are 2.9906 and 0.4019, so the inflows are worth 1,050,000 × 2.9906 = 3,140,130 plus 550,000 × 0.4019 = 221,045, which is 3,140,130 + 221,045 = 3,361,175, and the net present value is 3,400,000 − 3,361,175 = 38,825 negative. The rate lies between the two.
Step 3. Interpolate. The total swing between the two trials is 40,930 + 38,825 = 79,755, and the positive part is 40,930 ÷ 79,755 = 0.513 of it, so the rate is 19 + 0.51 = 19.51 per cent.
Check. Call it about 19.5 per cent against a hurdle of 9 per cent, which is comfortable, and note that it is the same conclusion the net present value reached. For a conventional project it always is.
Its advantages. It is a rate, so it can be compared with the cost of capital, with other projects and with returns outside the business without any further work; it communicates to people who will not sit through a discounting explanation; it needs no discount rate as an input, which is useful where the cost of capital is disputed; and the gap between it and the hurdle rate is a visible margin of safety.
Its limitations, and these are heavily examined.
The reinvestment assumption. The arithmetic implicitly assumes that every interim cash flow is reinvested at the internal rate of return itself. For a project returning 19.5 per cent that assumes Orontes can keep finding 19.5 per cent uses for the money, which is a strong claim; net present value assumes reinvestment at the cost of capital, which is far more defensible because the company can always repay debt or return cash at that rate.
It ignores scale, being a rate. Section 16.4 shows what that does.
It can have more than one value, or none at all, where the flows change sign more than once. Section 16.5 deals with that.
It cannot be averaged or added across projects, because rates on different amounts do not combine.
