16.4  Where the two measures disagree
For a single project considered on its own, the two measures never disagree about acceptance. They disagree about ranking, and only where projects are mutually exclusive — where taking one means not taking the other, because they use the same site, the same licence, the same machine or the same scarce manager.
Three causes, and the exam asks for them by name.
Scale. A small project can have a very high rate and create little value.
Timing. A project whose flows arrive early has a higher internal rate of return than one of the same size whose flows arrive late, even where the late one creates more value in total.
Life. A short project and a long one of the same size rank differently by rate and by value, for the same reason.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: a high rate on a small project
Step 1. Two projects, one site. Project S is a repacking contract: 1,000,000 out now and 1,400,000 back in a year. Project L is the roasting line of section 16.1. Only one can occupy the free floor space.
Step 2. Project S by rate. The money grows 1,400,000 ÷ 1,000,000 = 1.40 times in one year, so its internal rate of return is 40 per cent — more than twice the line's 19.5 per cent.
Step 3. Project S by value. Discounting at 9 per cent, 1,400,000 ÷ 1.09 = 1,284,404, so the net present value is 1,284,404 − 1,000,000 = 284,404.
Step 4. Compare. Project S: 40 per cent, 284,404 of value. Project L: 19.5 per cent, 1,041,630 of value. The rate prefers S and the value prefers L.
Check. Take L. Orontes is not trying to earn the highest rate on the smallest sum; it is trying to be worth more. The missing 1,041,630 − 284,404 = 757,226 is the cost of letting a percentage make the decision — and the honest way to see it is to ask what Orontes would do with the 2,400,000 it did not invest in Project S, which is to put it to work at the cost of capital, where by definition it creates nothing.
That last sentence is the whole argument. A rate tells you how hard a dollar works; a value tells you how much money the project makes. When the two conflict, net present value is correct, because the company's objective is to be worth more and not to maximise a ratio.
Two supplementary points the exam uses.
The crossover rate. Plotting both projects' net present values against the discount rate gives two curves that cross. Below the crossover rate one project is better; above it the other is. Where the hurdle rate sits on the wrong side of the crossover, the rate ranking misleads, which is exactly the situation above.
The incremental approach. Where a rate answer is wanted anyway, compute the internal rate of return on the difference between the two projects — the extra 2,400,000 invested and the extra flows it brings — and accept the larger project if that incremental rate beats the hurdle. It reaches the same conclusion as net present value, with more work.
Figure F716-03 sets the comparison out.
Figure F716-03. Rate against value: the repacking contract and the roasting line
@table
Measure | Project S (repacking) | Project L (roasting line) | Prefers
Investment | 1,000,000 | 3,400,000 | —
Internal rate of return | 40 per cent | 19.5 per cent | S
Net present value at 9% | 284,404 | 1,041,630 | L
Correct choice | | Take L | Value, not rate
@end
