16.5  Multiple rates, no rate, and the modified internal rate of return
A conventional project has one sign change — out, then in — and exactly one internal rate of return. Where the flows change sign more than once the equation can have several roots, or none.
Projects with more than one sign change are common: a mine or quarry that must be restored at the end, a plant with a large decommissioning cost, a project needing a second injection of capital in the middle, a contract with a penalty on completion.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: a project with two internal rates of return
Step 1. The flows. A sesame-hulling site costs 1,000,000 now, returns 2,600,000 at the end of year 1, and requires 1,650,000 of restoration at the end of year 2. Two sign changes.
Step 2. Test 10 per cent. The inflow is worth 2,600,000 ÷ 1.1 = 2,363,636 and the restoration 1,650,000 ÷ 1.21 = 1,363,636, so the net of the two is 2,363,636 − 1,363,636 = 1,000,000, which exactly offsets the initial outlay. The net present value is zero, so 10 per cent is an internal rate of return.
Step 3. Test 50 per cent. Now the inflow is worth 2,600,000 ÷ 1.5 = 1,733,333 and the restoration 1,650,000 ÷ 2.25 = 733,333, so the net is 1,733,333 − 733,333 = 1,000,000 and the net present value is zero again. Fifty per cent is also an internal rate of return.
Step 4. So what. Both are mathematically correct and neither is a return in any useful sense. The question "is the internal rate of return above the 9 per cent hurdle?" has two contradictory answers, so the measure has failed.
Check. Net present value has not failed. At 9 per cent the value is 2,600,000 ÷ 1.09 = 2,385,321 for the inflow and 1,650,000 ÷ 1.1881 = 1,388,772 for the restoration, so the project's value is 2,385,321 − 1,388,772 = 996,549 against an outlay of 1,000,000 — very slightly negative, and the decision is to reject, by the narrowest of margins. One rate, one answer.
The no-rate case is rarer and also real: some flow patterns give no real solution at all, so there is simply no internal rate of return to compute.
The modified internal rate of return repairs the reinvestment problem and, incidentally, the multiple-rate one. It works in three steps: compound every inflow forward to the end of the project at the cost of capital, discount every outflow back to time zero at the same rate, and find the single rate that links the two. Because only one sign change survives, there is exactly one answer.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: the roasting line's modified rate
Step 1. Compound the inflows at 9 per cent. The five annual flows of 1,050,000, compounded forward to the end of year 5, carry factors of 1.41158, 1.29503, 1.18810, 1.09000 and 1.00000, which sum to 5.98471, so they are worth 1,050,000 × 5.98471 = 6,283,946 at the end.
Step 2. Add the terminal flow. It already falls at the end of year 5, so 6,283,946 + 550,000 = 6,833,946.
Step 3. Link to the outlay. The investment of 3,400,000 grows to 6,833,946 over five years, a multiple of 6,833,946 ÷ 3,400,000 = 2.010.
Step 4. Take the fifth root. The fifth root of 2.010 is 1.1499, so the modified internal rate of return is 15.0 per cent.
Check. Fifteen per cent against the unmodified 19.5 per cent. The difference is the whole of the reinvestment assumption: the plain internal rate of return credited the project with reinvesting its interim flows at 19.5 per cent, and the modified measure credits it only with 9 per cent, which is what Orontes can actually get. The project still clears the hurdle, by a smaller and more honest margin.
Figure F716-04 summarises the four measures of this chapter.
Figure F716-04. The measures compared
@table
Measure | Reinvests interim flows at | Unique answer | Reflects size | Verdict on the line
Net present value | Cost of capital | Always | Yes | 1,041,630 — accept
Internal rate of return | Itself | Not always | No | 19.5 per cent — accept
Modified internal rate | Cost of capital | Always | No | 15.0 per cent — accept
Crossover analysis | n/a | n/a | n/a | Explains conflicts
@end
