Chapter summary
Figure F716-06 brings the chapter together.
Figure F716-06. Chapter 16 summary map: the key rule for each section.
Net present value is the present value of the inflows less the present value of the outflows at the hurdle rate, and for the roasting line it was 4,084,185 + 357,445 = 4,441,630 against an outlay of 3,400,000, leaving 1,041,630 of value created. Its advantages are that it uses every cash flow, discounts properly, makes the required return explicit, measures in money and is additive; its limitations are its dependence on forecasts and on the rate, its difficulty of explanation, and its silence about scale and liquidity. The internal rate of return is the rate at which that value is zero — here 19 + 0.51 = 19.51 per cent, found by interpolating between trials at 19 and 20 per cent — and it communicates well, needs no cost of capital, and assumes interim flows are reinvested at itself, which is usually indefensible. The two never disagree about accepting a single project and can disagree about ranking mutually exclusive ones, through scale, timing and life: the repacking contract's 40 per cent on 1,000,000 created only 284,404 of value against the line's 1,041,630, and the value is right. And where flows change sign more than once there can be two rates or none, as the hulling site's 10 and 50 per cent showed, while the modified internal rate of return — 6,833,946 ÷ 3,400,000 = 2.010, fifth root 1.1499, so 15.0 per cent — compounds at the cost of capital and always gives exactly one.
