D.	19.5 per cent
Case-style practice: the licence
Orontes can bid for one of two five-year licences at the Latakia port, and can hold only one. Licence A needs 800,000 of equipment and returns 320,000 a year for five years. Licence B needs 3,400,000 — it is the roasting line, relocated — and returns 1,050,000 a year plus 550,000 at the end. The cost of capital is 9 per cent, where the five-year annuity factor is 3.8897 and the year-5 factor 0.6499. Licence A's internal rate of return is 28.6 per cent; Licence B's is 19.5 per cent.
Required. Compute Licence A's net present value, say which licence you would bid for, and explain to a non-financial director why the licence with the lower rate may be the right one. Under 300 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC16-1  Answer A. Zero means the project exactly covers its cost of capital.
B is wrong. It earns exactly the required return, which is not nothing.
C is wrong. It is acceptable, if marginal.
D is wrong. The flows exist; they discount to the outlay.
SC16-2  Answer A. 1,050,000 × 3.8897 = 4,084,185.
B is wrong. That is the undiscounted total.
C is wrong. That is the outlay.
D is wrong. That is a single-year figure.
SC16-3  Answer A. Money measures add; rates do not.
B is wrong. The rate is central to it.
C is wrong. Forecasts are essential.
D is wrong. It is stated in money.
SC16-4  Answer A. It says nothing about return relative to investment.
B is wrong. It uses all flows, which is a strength.
C is wrong. Discounting is its method.
D is wrong. Uneven flows are handled year by year.
SC16-5  Answer A. It measures value created for capital providers.
B is wrong. Accounting profit is not its basis.
C is wrong. It ignores the pattern of recovery.
D is wrong. Tax enters the flows, not the objective.
SC16-6  Answer A. The output cannot be more precise than the input.
B is wrong. The precision is spurious.
C is wrong. The arithmetic may be perfect.
D is wrong. Conservatism is a different matter.
SC16-7  Answer A. Zero net present value defines it.
B is wrong. The greatest value is at the lowest rate.
C is wrong. Payback is a different measure.
D is wrong. Accounting break-even is unrelated.
SC16-8  Answer A. 40,930 ÷ 79,755 = 0.513, so about 19.5 per cent.
B is wrong. At 19 per cent the value is still positive.
C is wrong. At 20 per cent it is negative.
D is wrong. That is not a rate in this range.
SC16-9  Answer A. It implicitly reinvests at its own rate.
B is wrong. That is net present value's assumption.
C is wrong. No measure here assumes the risk-free rate.
D is wrong. The flows are assumed to earn something.
SC16-10  Answer A. The three classic causes.
B is wrong. Errors would affect both measures alike.
C is wrong. Tax is inside the flows.
D is wrong. Inflation is one input among many.
SC16-11  Answer A. The objective is to be worth more.
B is wrong. A rate ignores size.
C is wrong. That is not a rule.
D is wrong. Payback ignores later flows entirely.
SC16-12  Answer A. 1,041,630 − 284,404 = 757,226.
B is wrong. That is S's own value.
C is wrong. The difference in rates is not the loss.
D is wrong. The loss is substantial.
SC16-13  Answer A. More than one sign change permits multiple roots.
B is wrong. Unevenness alone is harmless.
C is wrong. Length is irrelevant.
D is wrong. The hurdle rate is not part of the computation.
SC16-14  Answer A. Compounding forward at the cost of capital is its definition.
B is wrong. That is the assumption it removes.
C is wrong. Zero would understate the flows.
D is wrong. The cost of capital, not the risk-free rate, is used.
Your turn
@table
Item | Answer
Present value of five flows of 1,050,000 | 4,084,185: 1,050,000 × 3.8897 = 4,084,185.
Present value of 550,000 in year 5 | 357,445: 550,000 × 0.6499 = 357,445.
Net present value | 1,041,630: 4,441,630 − 3,400,000 = 1,041,630.
Decision on that basis | Accept: the value is positive, so the project returns more than the money costs.
Rate at which net present value is zero | About 19.5 per cent, by interpolation between the two trials.
Which measure wins a conflict | Net present value, because the objective is value and not a ratio.
What it tells you | The two measures were computed from one set of flows and one rate. Everything else about them concerns what each one leaves out: scale, timing, reinvestment and the number of answers.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P16-01  Answer A. Inflows less outflows, both discounted.
B is wrong. Undiscounted totals ignore timing.
C is wrong. Accounting profit is not cash.
D is wrong. That describes the internal rate of return.
P16-02  Answer A. 4,084,185 + 357,445 = 4,441,630.
B is wrong. That omits the terminal flow.
C is wrong. That discounts nothing.
D is wrong. That is the net figure after the outlay.
P16-03  Answer A. 4,441,630 − 3,400,000 = 1,041,630.
B is wrong. That is before the outlay.
C is wrong. This adds instead of subtracting.
D is wrong. That is a ratio, not a value.
P16-04  Answer A. Zero means exactly the required return.
B is wrong. The required return is earned in full.
C is wrong. The flows are not zero.
D is wrong. Zero is acceptable.
P16-05  Answer A. It reinvests at its own rate.
B is wrong. That is net present value, and the modified rate.
C is wrong. Neither measure uses it.
D is wrong. Inflation is handled in the flows or the rate.
P16-06  Answer A. 40,930 ÷ 79,755 = 0.513 of the one-point gap.
B is wrong. That understates the interpolation.
C is wrong. That overstates it.
D is wrong. Above 20 per cent the value is negative.
P16-07  Answer A. Rates on different amounts do not combine.
B is wrong. Net present values are additive.
C is wrong. Cash flows add.
D is wrong. Investments add.
P16-08  Answer A. The three classic causes of conflict.
B is wrong. Tax affects both measures identically.
C is wrong. Depreciation affects the flows, not the conflict.
D is wrong. Working capital is one flow among several.
P16-09  Answer A. Two sign changes permit two roots.
B is wrong. One rate gives one value.
C is wrong. The flows exist.
D is wrong. Net present value is always computable.
P16-10  Answer A. The fifth root of 2.010 implies 15.0 per cent.
B is wrong. That is the multiple expressed as a rate.
C is wrong. That misreads the root.
D is wrong. That is the unmodified rate.
Case-style set: the licence
Licence A's net present value. The flows are level, so 320,000 × 3.8897 = 1,244,704, and against an outlay of 800,000 the value is 1,244,704 − 800,000 = 444,704.
Licence B's net present value. From section 16.1, 1,041,630.
Which to bid for. Licence B. It creates 1,041,630 − 444,704 = 596,926 more value than Licence A, and value is what the company is trying to create.
Explaining it to a non-financial director. The rate answers a different question from the one we are asking. Licence A's 28.6 per cent says that every dollar we put into it works harder than every dollar in Licence B — and that is true. But we are not short of dollars; we are short of licences, and we can hold only one. The question is therefore not "which uses a dollar best?" but "which makes us more money?", and Licence B makes us about 597,000 more.
Put the other way: if we take Licence A we save 2,600,000 of capital, and the best we can do with it is earn our cost of capital, which by definition adds nothing. So the saved capital does not make up the difference. If a second licence were available on Licence A's terms, we would want both — but that is not the choice in front of us.
What I would want before bidding. The reliability of Licence B's volume forecast, since it carries five times the capital; whether the licence can be surrendered or sublet, which is Chapter 15's option to abandon; and what the port authority can change during the five years, because a licence is a promise by somebody else.
